title: The Lookout
start: start

== start ==
bg west_hall
music room_tone
The tower is thirty feet of timber on a ridge, and the stairs are open, and the
wind comes through them whatever the season. There is a bed, a stove, a radio
that works, and a map on the wall under a sheet of plastic with the whole
forest on it in green.
You have sat in this chair for eleven summers and you have called in four fires.
Four is a lot. Most lookouts go a career and call in none.
The log is a ruled book. Every half hour you write the time, the temperature,
the wind, and what you can see. What you can see is usually nothing.
-> the_first_hour

== the_first_hour ==
At 16:40 the radio does its half-hour check.
base: Lookout, base. Anything for us?
you: Nothing. Wind's up to twelve, gusting eighteen.
base: Copy. Out.
You write it down. Wind twelve, gusting eighteen, nothing to report.
At 17:20 there is smoke in the north-east quadrant, about four miles out, thin
and grey and going straight up, which is not what a campfire does.
-> decide

== decide ==
You know that quadrant. It is a stand of beetle-killed pine that the district
has been arguing about for three years, and it is exactly the kind of place
that burns hot and fast and cannot be walked into.
Four miles is under an hour for a ground crew and about eleven minutes for the
helicopter, which is at the airstrip with nobody in it until Friday.
* Call it in now, before you have talked yourself out of it. -> call_it
* Take the glass and look properly first. -> watch_it

== watch_it ==
You take the glass and you find it again and you hold it, and you do the
arithmetic you have done four times before: how much smoke, how fast, how much
of the day is left, how far the nearest water is.
It is going straight up, which is what it does when there is no wind on it yet,
and it is getting thicker at the base, which is what it does when it is
working its way down into the duff.
Nine minutes of watching tells you everything that four minutes would have,
which is the whole of what a lookout is for and also the whole of the risk.
* Radio it in with the column height and the drift. -> call_it
* Sit on it until the half-hour check. -> wait_for_check

== wait_for_check ==
The 17:50 check comes at 17:50.
base: Lookout, base.
you: Smoke in the north-east quadrant, four miles, beetle-kill stand. It is
going up and it is getting wider at the base.
base: Copy. We'll have somebody look at it in the morning.
you: In the morning it will be a hundred acres.
base: It'll be whatever it is. There's nobody to send tonight.
You put the radio down. You have done your job and you have written it down,
and the district will have a line in a file that says the lookout reported
smoke at 17:50 on the day it started.
~ called_late true
-> the_night

== call_it ==
you: Base, Lookout. I have smoke in the north-east quadrant, approximately four
miles, in the beetle-kill stand. Column is going vertical and widening at the
base. Requesting a ground crew and the helicopter.
There is a pause that is longer than the radio needs.
base: Lookout, that's the fourth time you've called one of those in from that
chair.
you: It is.
base: Copy. Ground crew rolling, ETA fifty minutes.
~ called_early true
-> the_night

== the_night ==
bg west_hall
The light goes at 20:10 and you do the last sweep by torch, which is not
required and which you have never missed.
The smoke is still there. It is not bigger, which means the crew found it, and
it is not gone, which means they are still on it.
At 21:30 the radio brings a voice you do not know, from the crew, which is not
how it usually goes - the district passes things on and the lookout hears about
it a week later in a summary.
crew: Lookout. We've got it at about two acres. We're going to be here all
night.
you: Copy.
crew: They said to say thanks. They said you're the one who calls them in from
that chair.
* "It was going up and widening. That's all." -> modesty
* "Somebody has to be up here when it starts." -> plainly

== modesty ==
you: It was going up and widening. That's all.
crew: That's all it ever is.
You write the time down. Temperature nine, wind dropping, smoke visible,
crews on scene. You write it in the same handwriting you have used for eleven
summers, and you shut the book, and you stay in the chair for another hour
because the tower is the only place you can see the glow.
-> the_morning

== plainly ==
you: Somebody has to be up here when it starts.
crew: Yes. Somebody does.
You write the time down. Temperature nine, wind dropping, smoke visible, crews
on scene. It is the fourth line like it in eleven years and it is the first one
you have ever written knowing the names of the people on the other end.
-> the_morning

== the_morning ==
bg west_hall
music fan_hum
At 06:00 base does the morning check, which is the district supervisor, which
means it is a Thursday.
base: Lookout. Status?
* Give the numbers. -> numbers
* Say what it was like. -> say_what [if called_early]
* Say what it cost. -> say_cost [if called_late]

== numbers ==
you: Temperature four, wind calm, visibility twenty miles. Two acres, crews on
scene, no structures. Nothing else to report.
base: Copy. Good work.
You write it down, which makes it true in the way that only a written thing is.
-> close_early

== say_what ==
you: It was going straight up when I found it, and by the time I had the glass
on it, it was widening at the base. It took nine minutes to be sure.
base: And you called it in at 17:29.
you: I did.
base: That's the earliest call from that chair in nine years. I looked it up.
You do not tell him you nearly waited. There is nothing in the log about the
nine minutes, and the log is the record.
-> close_early

== say_cost ==
you: It was four miles out at 17:20 and I called it at 17:50. In the morning it
is going to be whatever it is.
base: It's two acres. It stayed small.
you: It stayed small because somebody got lucky with the wind, not because
somebody got up here fast.
base: That's not what the file says.
you: I know what the file says. I wrote it.
-> close_late

== close_early ==
You close the book at the end of the shift and you put it on the shelf with the
others, and the shelf is eleven books long, and the newest one has a line in it
that says smoke reported 17:29.
The helicopter comes on Friday and does not get used.
You are back in the chair on Saturday.
-> END

== close_late ==
You close the book at the end of the shift and you put it on the shelf with the
others, and the shelf is eleven books long, and the newest one has a line in it
that says smoke reported 17:50.
The helicopter comes on Friday and does not get used, and the crew goes home,
and the district writes a summary that says the fire was held at two acres.
You are back in the chair on Saturday, because somebody has to be up here when
it starts, and because you have never once been sure that it was you.
-> END
